OPTION 2 – No technical key at all (last resort)

If your table truly has no key column, you can still mask, but:

The trigger will join back on a combination of columns.

If there are duplicate rows, they’ll all be masked the same – for DEV this is usually OK.

Example using all non-masked columns ColA, ColB, ColC as a pseudo key:

CREATE TRIGGER trg_DimPatient_Mask
ON dbo.DimPatient
AFTER INSERT, UPDATE
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    UPDATE p
    SET
        [DateOfBirth]        = DATEFROMPARTS(1950, 1, 1),
        [ReligiousAffiliation] = 'Unknown',
        [Address 1: Street 1]  = 'Masked Street',
        [Address 1: Street 2]  = NULL,
        [Address1: City]       = 'Masked City',
        [Address2: Zip]        = '99999',
        [PatientFirstName]     = 'MaskedFN',
        [PatientLastName]      = 'MaskedLN',
        [Primary Email]        = 'masked@example.com'
    FROM dbo.DimPatient p
    JOIN inserted i
      ON p.ColA = i.ColA
     AND p.ColB = i.ColB
     AND p.ColC = i.ColC;   -- use the columns that define a “row” in your DW
END;
GO



